 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.03333333333333333,
   "dur": 3.815,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Can one day outlast a year? On Venus: yes. And no."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.566666666666666,
   "dur": 9.138,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "On Earth, you see the Sun rise about three hundred sixty-five times a lap. Next door, Venus hides its face under a permanent lid of cloud."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 14.7,
   "dur": 11.733,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "For three hundred years, astronomers chased faint smudges in those clouds. The Cassinis, father and son, clocked about twenty-three hours. Schiaparelli said one side always faced the Sun."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.966666666666665,
   "dur": 12.7,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "In nineteen sixty-two, radar from a dish in California pierced the clouds. Both guesses were wrong. Venus turns once every two hundred forty-three days, longer than its year. And it turns backwards."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 42.43333333333333,
   "dur": 7.94,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Stand there, and the spin and the orbit team up. The Sun comes back every hundred and seventeen days, rising in the west."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 54.166666666666664,
   "dur": 6.057,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So yes: one turn outlasts a year. But the day you'd live by is the Sun coming back."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.1,
   "dur": 6.219,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Light time, Sun to Venus: six minutes. The radar's round trip, at its closest: about four minutes."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 68.03333333333333,
   "dur": 4.571,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: the Moon never stops falling toward us. So why doesn't it land?"
  }
 ]
}